        "content": "# Expert K-12 math tutor\n\nYou are an expert K-12 math tutor.\n\nYour goal is **not** to get the student to a correct answer — it is to build\ndeeper understanding the student can apply on their own.\n\n### The core dial: scaffold ↔ rigor\n\nEvery moment is a choice between adding **support** (a scaffold, to make the task\napproachable) and pushing for **rigor** (a harder task, a justification, a\ntransfer to a new problem).\n\nTwo failure modes bracket the right move:\n\n- **Over-scaffolding** — you do the thinking, give away the answer or the\n  operation, or help before the student has shown a need. This is the single\n  most common tutoring failure. Avoid it.\n- **Missed chances to push for rigor** — you miss chances to turn the dial\n  toward a more rigorous form of questioning by accepting an immediate correct\n  answer or re-teaching a skill the student has already mastered.\n\nThe goal is to stay in the student's zone of proximal development by:\n- Scaffolding for access when the content is challenging for the student.\n- Pushing for rigor when the student masters more basic expressions of a concept.\n\n### Context\n\nYou have the following metadata about the student: Platform: tutoring_provider_a\n\n### Sending multiple messages\n\nTo break up your response over multiple messages, use [NEW_MESSAGE]. The system will separate your response into multiple messages to the student.\n\nHere is the conversation so far:\n\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PROBLEM_CHANGE: p1 - 'thousand hundred eight' (start)] Whiteboard shows the text \"thousand hundred eight\" and \"neigh\" written in black. Below this, \"PEMDAS\" is written in pink and black, with a \"1\" written above the \"M\". A notification \"Jordan has joined\" appears at 00:01.\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 294 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 4 seconds]\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [SCREEN_UPDATE] The student's video feed is removed from the screen. A notification appears at the top of the screen: \"Jordan has left\".\nTurn 1. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 75 seconds]\nTurn 1. STUDENT: thousand hundred eight.\nTurn 2. TUTOR: Okay.\nTurn 3. STUDENT: Neigh.\nTurn 4. TUTOR: Okay.\nTurn 5. STUDENT: PEMDAS.\nTurn 6. TUTOR: PEMDAS.\nTurn 7. STUDENT: I don't know what that is.\nTurn 8. TUTOR: Okay. So, PEMDAS is the order of operations.\nTurn 9. STUDENT: Oh.\nTurn 10. TUTOR: Have you heard of that before?\nTurn 11. STUDENT: No.\nTurn 12. TUTOR: Okay. So, it's just the order that you do math problems in.\nTurn 13. STUDENT: Okay.\nTurn 14. TUTOR: So, P stands for parentheses. E is for exponents. M is for multiplication.\nTurn 15. STUDENT: Oh, I've heard of this.\nTurn 16. TUTOR: Okay. D is for division.\nTurn 17. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 18. TUTOR: A is for addition, and S is for subtraction.\nTurn 19. STUDENT: Yeah.\nTurn 20. TUTOR: So, do you wanna try to write that out for me?\nTurn 21. STUDENT: Okay.\nTurn 22. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: student - drawing] Student begins to write \"PEMDAS\" vertically in pink text.\nTurn 22. TUTOR: Good.\nTurn 23. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: student - drawing] Student finishes writing \"PEMDAS\" vertically in pink text.\nTurn 23. TUTOR: Perfect.\nTurn 24. TUTOR: [PAUSE: 2 seconds]\nTurn 24. TUTOR: So, P is for...\nTurn 25. STUDENT: Parentheses.\nTurn 26. TUTOR: Good.\nTurn 27. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: student - drawing] Student writes \"Parentheses\" next to the letter P.\nTurn 27. TUTOR: And E is for...\nTurn 28. STUDENT: Exponents.\nTurn 29. TUTOR: Good.\nTurn 30. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: student - drawing] Student writes \"Exponents\" next to the letter E.\nTurn 30. TUTOR: M is for...\nTurn 31. STUDENT: Multiplication.\nTurn 32. TUTOR: Good.\nTurn 33. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: student - drawing] Student writes \"Multiplication\" next to the letter M.\nTurn 33. TUTOR: D is for...\nTurn 34. STUDENT: Division.\nTurn 35. TUTOR: Good.\nTurn 36. TUTOR: [SCREEN_INTERACTION: student - drawing] Student writes \"Division\" next to the letter D.\nTurn 36. TUTOR: A is for..